DEF("case:div-zero", S2(A("/"), S2(N(4), N(0)))),
DEF("case:comp", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), A("tl")), S3(A("a"), A("b"), A("c")))),
DEF("case:cons", S2(S3(A("CONS"), N(2), N(1)), S2(A("p"), A("q")))),
DEF("case:const", S2(S2(A("CONST"), A("k")), A("ignored"))),
DEF("case:cond", S2(S4(A("COND"), A("null"), S2(A("CONST"), A("empty")), S2(A("CONST"), A("full"))), PHI())),
DEF("case:alpha", S2(S2(A("ALPHA"), A("tl")), S2(S2(A("a"), A("b")), S2(A("c"), A("d"))))),
DEF("case:insert-max", S2(S2(A("INSERT"), A("system:max2")), S4(A("2"), A("19"), A("7"), A("4")))),
DEF("case:while-count", S2(S3(A("WHILE"), S3(A("COMP"), A("gt"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), S2(A("CONST"), N(0)))), S3(A("COMP"), A("-"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), S2(A("CONST"), N(1))))), N(5))),
DEF("case:bu", S2(S3(A("BU"), A("+"), N(10)), N(7))),
DEF("case:const-bottom", S2(S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("CONST"), A("k")), N(1)), PHI())),
"Codd 1970 1.3 via arest's own ruling (the rmap:schema note): the flat table is the DERIVED PRESENTATION rmap:unnest, not a stored form. engine/rust computes that projection in 442 lines of Rust (op_sql_project) with NO test anywhere, so the canon path that ought to replace it had no oracle on either lineage. These two give it one. The first is a single role level; the second nests a level inside a level, which is the curry (one store level per role) and is where unnest recurses through rmap:unnest_cell -- the recursion the DEFS lookup resolves, so no host owes a loop.",
DEF("case:rmap-unnest", S2(A("rmap:unnest"),
    S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("Ada"), S1(A("x"))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("Bob"), S1(A("y")))))),
DEF("case:rmap-unnest-curried", S2(A("rmap:unnest"),
    S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("Ada"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("age"), S1(A("42")))))))),
"store:namerows against engine/rust's `cells` op: unsorted input, one multi-row cell and one single-row cell, so the case pins BOTH the counting and the sort. The third cell is an ATOM cell -- no population -- and must answer bottom, which is the `null` rows field engine/rust emits.",
"ast:Store IS down-n, THE state transition operator of an AST system, and it had NO CASE. Backus 13.3.4 defines it as pop then push: down-n applied to <x,y> removes the FIRST cell named n from y and prepends a fresh one -- the formal definition is push-n = apndl of the new CELL triple, and the text calls it pushing the top of a STACK named n. engine/rust reimplements it natively in store_into, which is NOT in the twin registry, the same omission theta:NatJoin had. -replace shows the pop and the prepend together: F1 comes back at the FRONT, not in place. -absent shows the degenerate arm, a plain prepend when there is nothing to pop. -STACK IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS: two cells share the name, only the FIRST is popped, and the deeper one SURVIVES underneath where up-n will never see it but a later pop will. That is the property store:replace_cell destroys by removing ALL cells of the name, and casing it here is what makes the difference between the two disciplines checkable rather than merely described.",
DEF("case:ast-store-replace", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:Store"), S2(A("CONST"), A("F1"))), A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S1(A("z"))),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"), S1(S1(A("a")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("F2"), S1(S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:ast-store-absent", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:Store"), S2(A("CONST"), A("F3"))), A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S1(A("z"))),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"), S1(S1(A("a")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("F2"), S1(S1(A("b")))))))),
DEF("case:ast-store-stack", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:Store"), S2(A("CONST"), A("F1"))), A("id"))),
    S2(S1(S1(A("z"))),
       S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"), S1(S1(A("new")))), S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"), S1(S1(A("old")))))))),

"A CELL MAY CONTAIN ANOTHER ENTIRE STORE -- Backus 14.7, and AREST.tex's tenancy and self-similarity claims already rest on it. ast:FetchPop now looks inside the FILE cell when the flat lookup answers the default. THE MEASUREMENT THAT MOTIVATES IT: the base carries 965 cells and 958 of them are POPULATIONS, siblings in D, while FILE sits there not holding them. Backus 14.4.1 says D is the set of DEFINITIONS; 14.4.2 says an update computes the whole new file and stores it with ONE down-FILE, the rest of the state unchanged; 14.6a says a state transition happens once per MAJOR COMPUTATION and that keeping transitions out of the tiniest details is how the von Neumann bottleneck goes away. Populations as siblings makes every up-n a scan of 958 entries, which is why NEval needs a name index at all and why a projection over a thousand cells takes ninety-five seconds. -nested is the new capability and -shadow is the constraint: a name present BOTH flat and in FILE resolves flat, because up-n takes the FIRST match and stage two must not change what stage one already answers. ast:Fetch itself is untouched -- it fetches DEFINITIONS too, and only the population accessor nests.",
DEF("case:fetchpop-flat", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:FetchPop"), S2(A("CONST"), A("F1"))), A("id"))),
    S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))))))),
DEF("case:fetchpop-nested", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:FetchPop"), S2(A("CONST"), A("F1"))), A("id"))),
    S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"),
          S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"), S2(S1(A("a")), S1(A("b"))))))))),
DEF("case:fetchpop-shadow", S2(
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:FetchPop"), S2(A("CONST"), A("F1"))), A("id"))),
    S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"), S1(S1(A("x")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("FILE"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("F1"), S1(S1(A("a"))))))))),

DEF("case:store-namerows", S2(A("store:namerows"),
    S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("Bob"), S2(A("x"), A("y"))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("Ada"), S1(A("z"))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("Cyd"), A("atom"))))),
"rmap:wide_row is the 3NF ENTITY-TABLE PIVOT -- id, then one slot per absorbed column -- which engine/rust rebuilds in op_sql_project (the `rows.push(row)` loop over per-column maps). The second case is the one that matters and is why this could not simply be transliterated: engine/rust writes SQL NULL as bottom INSIDE the row (`unwrap_or_else(bot)`), which its Vec<V> tolerates but Backus 13.2 does not -- a sequence containing bottom IS bottom, so that row would be no row at all. Canon answers an ABSENT slot with phi and a present one with a non-empty slot (rmap:lookup0), the option encoding, and the host renders phi as null. Same boundary store:namerows hit. The -mixed case is the one that proves it usable: one present column and one absent column answer <'a',<<'x'>>,phi> -- ARITY PRESERVED, one slot per column, which is what a table row has to be. The -null case alone could not show that, since a single absent column and a ragged row look alike from one row.",
DEF("case:rmap-wide-row", S2(A("rmap:wide_row"),
    S2(A("a"),
       S1(S5(A("F1"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Widget")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(),
             S1(S2(A("a"), A("x")))))))),
DEF("case:rmap-wide-row-null", S2(A("rmap:wide_row"),
    S2(A("zz"),
       S1(S5(A("F1"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Widget")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(),
             S1(S2(A("a"), A("x")))))))),
"rmap:ddl_table against the two forms engine/rust emits (main.rs 10827, 10949). The entity form carries its key on a column's own type and passes phi keycols; the relation form passes the key columns and gets the trailing clause. The second case also carries a REFERENCES column, which is the arm the Rust splits in two and canon does not.",
"rmap:ddl_name's two arms. The guard case is the real defect ddl._sql_name records: 'SQLite Fact Base' slugs to sqlite_fact_base, which SQLite refuses as a reserved namespace, so it must come back t_sqlite_fact_base. The plain case must NOT be prefixed -- a guard that fires on everything would pass a test that only checked the guard.",
"rmap:ddl_order over all three ways a table can be ready -- no parents at all (parent), a parent that is not a projected table (orphan/external), and a self-reference (self) -- with one table (child) that must WAIT for its parent. Ready tables leave sorted per wave, so wave 1 is orphan,parent,self and child follows: a globally sorted answer would put child first and is the wrong answer. The cycle case is the terminating arm: a and b each wait on the other, nothing is ever ready, and the remainder goes out sorted instead of looping forever.",
"rmap:coldisamb: the FIRST 'a' keeps its name and the later ones become a_2, a_3 -- suffixing every duplicate including the first would also produce distinct names and would be the wrong answer, so the case pins which one keeps the base. 'b' and 'c' occur once and must come back untouched.",
"rmap:colsof over rmapColumns rows given OUT of column order and with another table's row mixed in. The answer must be ftA,ftC -- ftA is written last but numbered 2, so a version that kept cell order would answer ftC,ftA and the DDL's columns would stop matching the rows. The 'other' row must not appear at all.",
"rmap:rolesof with the roles given out of order and another fact type's role mixed in. The tie case pins what canon does when two roles share a position -- engine/rust sorted the (pos,player) tuple, canon orders by position alone, and a position is unique within its fact type so the store never presents this; pinned rather than assumed.",
"rmap:rolegroups: F1 must come before F2 because F1's role is seen first, even though F2 sorts earlier -- first-seen order, not alphabetical, is what the entity sweep walks.",
"rmap:refmode's two precedences, each with the name present TWICE so a uniform rule cannot pass. Person is in refScheme twice: LAST wins (nr, not code). Car is in refMode twice and absent from refScheme: FIRST wins (vin, not plate). Van is in neither: id. Person also appears in refMode, which must be ignored entirely -- refScheme having spoken settles it.",
"rmap:entities keeps only the ObjectType rows. The -short case is the one worth having: a one-element row must be SKIPPED, exactly as engine/rust's it.len() >= 2 skips it. Without the length guard 2 of that row is bottom and the whole sweep answers bottom instead of losing one row -- a failure that looks nothing like the one row it came from.",
"rmap:sortnames over bare atoms -- the case exists because system:sort_asc answers bottom on them, which is what ord_names hit. owntables drops the absorbed fact type and sorts what is left (F2 is written before F1 in the rows, so an unsorted answer shows). entitytables keeps every entity and adds the absorbing tables that are not own fact types -- F2 is own, so it must NOT appear.",
"rmap:playerpos keeps only the roles the table itself plays: F1 names Person at position 1 and Car at 2, F2 names only Car, so Person answers <F1,1> alone. rmap:atpos selects position 2 dynamically through apply and SKIPS the short row -- without the guard that row is bottom and the whole projection is bottom, not one value short.",
"rmap:valpairs drops the row that cannot carry both an id and a value, and keeps ROW ORDER -- the duplicate id is emitted twice on purpose, because the last-wins rule lives in the host's map (keyed by its own value identity) and canon must not pre-empt it by silently keeping one.",
"colfts and coltables over the same rmapColumns rows: ftA is absorbed by two different tables and person absorbs two fact types, so both projections must dedup. Order is dedup's last-occurrence and is pinned rather than relied on -- one answer feeds a setminus exclusion, the other a set.",
"The rule split: r2 is an aggregate so it must land in the agg list and NOT the plain one, and the one-element row r3 must land in NEITHER -- a row without a head cell names no derivation. Both lists come from the same fold over the same rows, so a case that only checked the plain list would miss an aggregate leaking into the closure, which is the exact failure the split exists to prevent (an aggregate head supersedes rather than unions).",
DEF("case:aggids", S2(A("derive:aggids"),
    S2(S2(A("r2"), A("count")), S1(A("r5"))))),
DEF("case:rules-plain", S2(A("derive:rules_plain"),
    S2(S1(A("r2")),
       S3(S2(A("r1"), A("H1")), S2(A("r2"), A("H2")), S1(A("r3")))))),
DEF("case:rules-agg", S2(A("derive:rules_agg"),
    S2(S1(A("r2")),
       S3(S2(A("r1"), A("H1")), S2(A("r2"), A("H2")), S1(A("r3")))))),
"derive:rulesplit must answer <plain, agg> -- the SAME two lists the separate folds give, from one pass. Pinned beside them so the one-pass form cannot drift from the two-pass one it replaced.",
DEF("case:rulesplit", S2(A("derive:rulesplit"),
    S2(S1(A("r2")),
       S3(S2(A("r1"), A("H1")), S2(A("r2"), A("H2")), S1(A("r3")))))),
DEF("case:colfts", S2(A("rmap:colfts"),
    S3(S3(A("person"), N(2), A("ftA")),
       S3(A("person"), N(3), A("ftC")),
       S3(A("car"), N(2), A("ftA"))))),
DEF("case:coltables", S2(A("rmap:coltables"),
    S3(S3(A("person"), N(2), A("ftA")),
       S3(A("person"), N(3), A("ftC")),
       S3(A("car"), N(2), A("ftA"))))),
"rmap:pivot_row over one row present and one absent, with all three cell shapes in each: a unary column, a functional column carrying the SAME id twice (so the LAST value must win -- z, not x), and a functional column that has nothing for this id (phi, the SQL NULL). The -absent case takes an id in none of them and must still answer a row of the SAME ARITY -- 0 and two phis, never a short row, because a table row has one cell per column.",
"rmap:take of more than a sequence holds must answer the whole sequence, not bottom -- the -over case. rmap:takerows SKIPS the row too short to bind its roles (engine/rust's `narrow`) and truncates the one that is too long, so the case carries one of each beside a row that already fits: a version that padded instead of skipping would answer three rows where two is right.",
"theta:firstseen beside theta:dedup on the SAME input, because the difference is the whole point. On <a,b,a,c,b>: dedup answers <a,c,b> -- each value's LAST occurrence, in the POSITION order of those last occurrences (a at 3, c at 4, b at 5) -- and firstseen answers <a,b,c>. The prose here first claimed dedup gave <b,c,a>; the case said otherwise, which is why both are pinned rather than one described. engine/rust's id sweep keeps the FIRST id it meets, so only firstseen is the rule it needs.",
"theta:aton against engine/rust's num(). The round trip is the point: aton of ntoa's answer must be the number back. '007' pins that leading zeros are digits, not a format. '-42' pins the sign arm. The three REFUSALS are what keep this honest -- '1.5' is a float and must come back as TEXT (canon does not imitate a platform float parser, the same rule that stopped set_key moving), '12a' is not a digit run, and the bare '-' has no magnitude after the sign and must not answer 0.",
"theta:groupby1: r1 appears twice with different values and must gather BOTH, in row order; r2 comes second because its first row is second, not because it sorts later. The one-element row is skipped, not padded -- and it sits in the middle so a version that stopped at the first bad row would lose r1's second value too.",
"derive:ftr_pairs swaps each role row to <ft, roleid> and keeps the FIRST of any duplicate. The repeat of <r1,F1> must collapse to one pair, and the two rows sharing F1 with DIFFERENT role ids must both survive -- deduping on the ft alone would lose the second, which is a fact type losing a role.",
"derive:uniq_constraints keeps BOTH uniqueness kinds and drops everything else. The mandatory row must not survive -- a restrict that matched any constraint would pass a case carrying only uniqueness rows -- and the two-element row is skipped rather than answering bottom for the whole restrict.",
"derive:copy_pairs takes elements 2 and 3, NOT 1 and 2 -- the row's first field is the rule id and is not part of the inclusion. The two-element row is skipped rather than answering bottom. The repeated pair is emitted TWICE on purpose: the caller collects these into a set, and collapsing them here would pre-empt a decision that call site has already made.",
"derive:txn_unambiguous carries all three rules at once: t1 is defined twice in the SAME machine and survives (a repeat is not a conflict); t2 is defined in TWO machines and is excluded WHOLE, not resolved to either; and the row already carrying the txn: surrogate is skipped, because an earlier pass keyed it and it must not be re-derived. Dropping any one rule still passes a case testing only the others. The surrogate here reads txn:M9-t9, a stand-in, because nothing in THIS case tests the separator -- only the txn: prefix. The separator itself is cased under derive:txn_surrogate now that it can be: no escape for a control character is legal in all five lineages (\\xHH is rejected by java, \\uHHHH by rust, both established by compiling them), so canon writes \\xHH and the java composer rewrites it to java's own \\u00HH spelling.",
"derive:reads_hit across all four ways it can answer: an intersection in the MIDDLE of the read set (not the first cell, which a test checking only the head would pass), no intersection at all, an EMPTY frontier, and a rule that READS NOTHING. The last two must both answer F -- an empty frontier waking every rule, or a rule with no reads waking on any change, are the two ways a wrong answer here would silently re-run the whole closure.",
"derive:atom_hits keeps the atoms whose FACT TYPE (element 2) is in the delta, not those whose kind matches -- the pair is <kind, fact type> and testing the wrong half is the mistake available here. ORDER is preserved: p1 before p4, as the rule declares them, because the join applies one ~d variant per hit in that order. The empty-delta case answers phi rather than every atom.",
DEF("case:atom-hits", S2(A("derive:atom_hits"),
    S2(S2(A("F1"), A("F3")),
       S4(S2(A("p1"), A("F1")), S2(A("p2"), A("F2")),
          S2(A("p3"), A("F9")), S2(A("p4"), A("F3")))))),
DEF("case:atom-hits-empty", S2(A("derive:atom_hits"),
    S2(PHI(), S2(S2(A("p1"), A("F1")), S2(A("p2"), A("F2")))))),
DEF("case:reads-hit", S2(A("derive:reads_hit"),
    S2(S2(A("A"), A("B")), S3(A("X"), A("B"), A("Y"))))),
DEF("case:reads-hit-miss", S2(A("derive:reads_hit"),
    S2(S1(A("A")), S2(A("X"), A("Y"))))),
DEF("case:reads-hit-nofrontier", S2(A("derive:reads_hit"),
    S2(PHI(), S1(A("X"))))),
DEF("case:reads-hit-noreads", S2(A("derive:reads_hit"),
    S2(S1(A("A")), PHI()))),
DEF("case:txn-unambiguous", S2(A("derive:txn_unambiguous"),
    S5(S2(A("t1"), A("M1")),
       S2(A("t2"), A("M1")),
       S2(A("t1"), A("M1")),
       S2(A("t2"), A("M2")),
       S2(A("txn:M9-t9"), A("M9"))))),
DEF("case:copy-pairs", S2(A("derive:copy_pairs"),
    S4(S3(A("r1"), A("A"), A("B")), S2(A("r2"), A("C")),
       S3(A("r3"), A("D"), A("E")), S3(A("r4"), A("A"), A("B"))))),
DEF("case:uniq-constraints", S2(A("derive:uniq_constraints"),
    S4(S3(A("c1"), A("uniqueness"), A("F1")),
       S3(A("c2"), A("mandatory"), A("F2")),
       S3(A("c3"), A("spanning_uniqueness"), A("F3")),
       S2(A("c4"), A("uniqueness"))))),
DEF("case:ftr-pairs", S2(A("derive:ftr_pairs"),
    S4(S2(A("r1"), A("F1")), S2(A("r2"), A("F2")),
       S2(A("r1"), A("F1")), S2(A("r3"), A("F1"))))),
"theta:groupby1_rest over THREE-wide rows, which is the case groupby1 cannot express: each group holds the whole rest of its row, <kind, cell>, not one value. The existing groupby1 case runs beside it unchanged, which is what says the specialisation still means what it meant when it was its own fold.",
DEF("case:groupby1-rest", S2(A("theta:groupby1_rest"),
    S4(S3(A("r1"), A("pos"), A("A")), S3(A("r2"), A("neg"), A("B")),
       S1(A("short")), S3(A("r1"), A("pos"), A("C"))))),
DEF("case:groupby1", S2(A("theta:groupby1"),
    S4(S2(A("r1"), A("A")), S2(A("r2"), A("B")), S1(A("short")), S2(A("r1"), A("C"))))),
DEF("case:aton", S2(A("theta:aton"), A("120"))),
DEF("case:aton-zeros", S2(A("theta:aton"), A("007"))),
DEF("case:aton-neg", S2(A("theta:aton"), A("-42"))),
DEF("case:aton-float", S2(A("theta:aton"), A("1.5"))),
DEF("case:aton-mixed", S2(A("theta:aton"), A("12a"))),
DEF("case:aton-baresign", S2(A("theta:aton"), A("-"))),
DEF("case:aton-plus", S2(A("theta:aton"), A("+5"))),
DEF("case:aton-bareplus", S2(A("theta:aton"), A("+"))),
DEF("case:aton-roundtrip", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:aton"), A("ntoa")), N(9042))),
DEF("case:firstseen", S2(A("theta:firstseen"), S5(A("a"), A("b"), A("a"), A("c"), A("b")))),
DEF("case:dedup-last", S2(A("theta:dedup"), S5(A("a"), A("b"), A("a"), A("c"), A("b")))),
"theta:union closes the set family, which could say setminus, seteq and subset but not union. The -order case is the one that matters: theta:dedup keeps the LAST occurrence, so a plain dedup.cat over <<a,b>,<a,c>> answers <b,a,c> and silently reorders the left side; firstseen.cat answers <a,b,c>. The three empty shapes are cased because cat and dedup each have their own phi edge.",
DEF("case:union", S2(A("theta:union"), S2(S2(A("a"), A("b")), S2(A("b"), A("c"))))),
DEF("case:union-order", S2(A("theta:union"), S2(S2(A("a"), A("b")), S2(A("a"), A("c"))))),
DEF("case:union-dup-left", S2(A("theta:union"), S2(S3(A("a"), A("a"), A("b")), S2(A("b"), A("c"))))),
DEF("case:union-empty-left", S2(A("theta:union"), S2(PHI(), S2(A("a"), A("b"))))),
DEF("case:union-empty-right", S2(A("theta:union"), S2(S2(A("a"), A("b")), PHI()))),
DEF("case:union-both-empty", S2(A("theta:union"), S2(PHI(), PHI()))),
"derive:txn_surrogate against rekey_transitions_native's format!. The operand is <name, smd> and the answer puts the SMD FIRST -- txn:{smd}US{name} -- because the key is scoped by its machine; a version that read the pair in operand order would answer txn:t9US M9 and every reference in the store would dangle. These cases carry no escape of their own: the separator lives once, in the DEF. -surrogates is the sweep, pairing each name with its surrogate so the host substitutes instead of formatting.",
DEF("case:txn-surrogate", S2(A("derive:txn_surrogate"), S2(A("t9"), A("M9")))),

"theta:Tie is the self-join tie: keep the rows whose FIRST element equals their LAST, then drop that last element, which is the duplicate the join introduced. It was reachable from no case and no law -- all four stations could have disagreed about it and every signal stayed green. The -none case is the one that matters: no row ties and the answer is phi, where an implementation that forgot the predicate would hand back every row with its last column shaved off, which still looks like a plausible relation.",
DEF("case:theta-tie", S2(A("theta:Tie"),
    S3(S3(A("a"), A("x"), A("a")), S3(A("b"), A("y"), A("c")),
       S3(A("d"), A("z"), A("d"))))),
DEF("case:theta-tie-none", S2(A("theta:Tie"),
    S2(S3(A("a"), A("x"), A("b")), S3(A("c"), A("y"), A("d"))))),
DEF("case:theta-tie-empty", S2(A("theta:Tie"), PHI())),

"theta:setminus had NO case, which is how a foundational set operation goes four hosts deep unchecked -- the same way `or` did. These pin the three properties a retract depends on and that a plausible wrong implementation would break. -all: a row present TWICE must go BOTH times, so an implementation removing the first match leaves one behind and the fact stays retracted-but-present. -order: the survivors keep the LEFT list's order, not the right's and not sorted, because protocol.py skips the rowsort on retract and the store would otherwise reshuffle on every retraction. -miss: retracting something absent is a no-op, not an error and not an empty answer. Rows are SEQUENCES here, so this also pins that equality is NATEQ over whole rows rather than identity.",
DEF("case:setminus", S2(A("theta:setminus"),
    S2(S3(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("b"), N(2)), S2(A("c"), N(3))),
       S1(S2(A("b"), N(2)))))),
DEF("case:setminus-all", S2(A("theta:setminus"),
    S2(S3(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("b"), N(2)), S2(A("a"), N(1))),
       S1(S2(A("a"), N(1)))))),
DEF("case:setminus-order", S2(A("theta:setminus"),
    S2(S3(A("c"), A("a"), A("b")), S1(A("a"))))),
DEF("case:setminus-miss", S2(A("theta:setminus"),
    S2(S2(A("a"), A("b")), S1(A("z"))))),
DEF("case:setminus-empty", S2(A("theta:setminus"), S2(PHI(), S1(A("a"))))),

"derive:txn_positions against rekey_transitions_native's pos_of loop. The answer is 0-BASED where the store is 1-based, which is the whole reason the DEF exists rather than a bare restrict-and-project: F1 at role position 2 answers 1. The -short case is a three-element row, which must be SKIPPED -- element 4 of it is bottom, and without the length guard eq of bottom would take the entire sweep down rather than one row (Backus 13.2). The -other case has no Transition-typed role at all and answers phi, not every row.",
DEF("case:txn-positions", S2(A("derive:txn_positions"),
    S3(S4(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(2), A("Transition")),
       S4(A("r2"), A("F2"), N(1), A("Status")),
       S4(A("r3"), A("F3"), N(3), A("Transition"))))),
DEF("case:txn-positions-short", S2(A("derive:txn_positions"),
    S2(S3(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(2)),
       S4(A("r2"), A("F2"), N(1), A("Transition"))))),
DEF("case:txn-positions-other", S2(A("derive:txn_positions"),
    S2(S4(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(2), A("Status")),
       S4(A("r2"), A("F2"), N(1), A("Role"))))),
DEF("case:txn-surrogates", S2(A("derive:txn_surrogates"),
    S2(S2(A("t9"), A("M9")), S2(A("t1"), A("M2"))))),

"rmap:atpos does NOT dedup and does NOT reorder, which is the property status_facts_native depends on -- its nouns come from a plain list comprehension and a governed noun named twice must be listed twice, because each one emits its own pair of readings. Cased separately from rmap:distinct_at precisely because the two differ only by the dedup, and picking the wrong one is a silent change in what gets compiled.",
DEF("case:atpos-dups", S2(A("rmap:atpos"),
    S2(N(2), S3(S2(A("m1"), A("Order")), S2(A("m2"), A("Order")),
                S2(A("m3"), A("Peer")))))),

"rmap:distinct_at at position 2, which is what create_handlers_native built a HashSet for. The -short case is why this is not just ALPHA 2: a row with no position 2 must DROP, not bottom the whole answer -- Backus 13.2 makes a sequence containing bottom bottom, so a selector applied blind would lose the population. The -coltables case re-runs the pos-1 instance the family already had, so redefining rmap:coltables in terms of this is held to its old answer.",
DEF("case:distinct-at2", S2(A("rmap:distinct_at"),
    S2(N(2), S3(S2(A("t1"), A("F1")), S2(A("t2"), A("F2")),
                S2(A("t3"), A("F1")))))),
DEF("case:distinct-at-short", S2(A("rmap:distinct_at"),
    S2(N(2), S3(S2(A("t1"), A("F1")), S1(A("t2")), S2(A("t3"), A("F3")))))),
DEF("case:distinct-at-empty", S2(A("rmap:distinct_at"), S2(N(2), PHI()))),

"rmap:abs_targets against layout_cells_native's HashSet-then-sort. The -self case is the one that earns its place: a fact type mapping to ITSELF absorbs nothing and must not appear, so a fold that projected element 2 unconditionally would answer <a,b> where the right answer is phi. The -unsorted case pins the sort, which the host got from calling .sort() after collecting a HashSet -- the set never had an order to preserve, so canon owes one.",
DEF("case:abs-targets", S2(A("rmap:abs_targets"),
    S4(S2(A("F1"), A("T1")), S2(A("F2"), A("T1")),
       S2(A("F3"), A("F3")), S2(A("F4"), A("T2"))))),
DEF("case:abs-targets-self", S2(A("rmap:abs_targets"),
    S2(S2(A("a"), A("a")), S2(A("b"), A("b"))))),
DEF("case:abs-targets-unsorted", S2(A("rmap:abs_targets"),
    S2(S2(A("F1"), A("T9")), S2(A("F2"), A("T1"))))),
DEF("case:abs-targets-empty", S2(A("rmap:abs_targets"), PHI())),

"store:drop_named and store:replace_cell against with_watermark_native (main.rs). replace_cell is the REMOVE-ALL-THEN-APPEND-LAST discipline, which is NOT Backus 13.3.4's pop-then-push that define_in uses -- that one removes only the FIRST match and PREPENDS, so the two must not be conflated and the -replaces case pins which. That -replaces and -appends answer identically is the upsert property itself: whether the name was already there stops being observable. drop-named-all answers phi, the arm a filter written as a guard gets wrong.",
DEF("case:drop-named", S2(A("store:drop_named"),
    S2(A("b"), S3(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("b"), N(2)), S2(A("c"), N(3)))))),
DEF("case:drop-named-missing", S2(A("store:drop_named"),
    S2(A("z"), S2(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("b"), N(2)))))),
DEF("case:drop-named-all", S2(A("store:drop_named"),
    S2(A("a"), S2(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("a"), N(2)))))),
DEF("case:replace-cell-replaces", S2(A("store:replace_cell"),
    S2(S3(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("b"), N(2)), S2(A("c"), N(3))),
       S2(A("b"), N(9))))),
DEF("case:replace-cell-appends", S2(A("store:replace_cell"),
    S2(S2(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("c"), N(3))), S2(A("b"), N(9))))),
DEF("case:replace-cell-empty", S2(A("store:replace_cell"),
    S2(PHI(), S2(A("a"), N(1))))),

"store:replace_cells is the same discipline applied to a SEQUENCE of pairs in one application, and the pair that makes it worth having is -order against -step2. -order hands two pairs to the batch; -step2 hands the SECOND pair alone to store:replace_cell, applied to the store -order should have after its first pair. They must print the same answer, on all four stations, or the batch is not the fold. That is the whole property: three rust sites carried the ordering in a HOST LOOP that marshalled the entire store across the boundary once per pair, and a loop that computes the right answer is still meaning living in the wrong place. -empty pins the identity arm, an empty pair sequence leaving the store untouched rather than answering phi, which is what a fold written as a filter gets wrong.",
DEF("case:replace-cells-order", S2(A("store:replace_cells"),
    S2(S3(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("b"), N(2)), S2(A("c"), N(3))),
       S2(S2(A("b"), N(9)), S2(A("a"), N(8)))))),
DEF("case:replace-cells-step2", S2(A("store:replace_cell"),
    S2(S3(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("c"), N(3)), S2(A("b"), N(9))),
       S2(A("a"), N(8))))),
DEF("case:replace-cells-one", S2(A("store:replace_cells"),
    S2(S3(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("b"), N(2)), S2(A("c"), N(3))),
       S1(S2(A("b"), N(9)))))),
DEF("case:replace-cells-empty", S2(A("store:replace_cells"),
    S2(S2(S2(A("a"), N(1)), S2(A("b"), N(2))), PHI()))),

"THE FIXPOINT HAD NO CASE. op_run_rules is 1268 lines, the largest single function in engine/rust and the one the rule-closure test spends fifteen minutes in, and canon already carries the whole machine it reimplements: derive:layers stratifies, derive:run_layer is the WHILE to a fixpoint, derive:round is one semi-naive round, derive:delta_eval evaluates a body against the delta. The host calls TEN of the 72 derive DEFs and every one of the ten is small preparation -- aggids, atom_hits, copy_pairs, ftr_pairs, reads_hit, rulesplit, txn_positions, txn_surrogates, txn_unambiguous, uniq_constraints. Not one of round, run_layer, layers, closure, stratified, eval or delta_eval was reached by any case on any station, so the canon side of the largest host function in the repo was unpinned meaning: present, and checked by nothing. derive:closure is the whole machine behind one operand -- it seeds a two-cell store from the edges, runs the transitive-closure rule pair through derive, and pops R -- so casing it exercises round, run_layer and delta_eval transitively. -chain is Bancilhon-Ramakrishnan's own example and -cycle is the one that separates a fixpoint from a single pass: with a cycle, every node reaches every node INCLUDING ITSELF, which one pass cannot produce and a loop that forgets to dedup never finishes.",
DEF("case:derive-closure-chain", S2(A("derive:closure"),
    S3(S2(A("a"), A("b")), S2(A("b"), A("c")), S2(A("c"), A("d"))))),
DEF("case:derive-closure-cycle", S2(A("derive:closure"),
    S3(S2(A("a"), A("b")), S2(A("b"), A("c")), S2(A("c"), A("a"))))),
DEF("case:derive-closure-single", S2(A("derive:closure"),
    S1(S2(A("a"), A("b"))))),
DEF("case:derive-closure-empty", S2(A("derive:closure"), PHI())),
DEF("case:derive-stratified-tc", S2(A("derive:stratified"),
    S2(S2(A("R"), S3(A("proj"), A("E"), S2(N(1), N(2)))),
       S2(A("R"), S4(A("join"), A("R"), A("R"), S2(N(1), N(3))))))),

"derive:mirror_fill, the asserted-wins rule both op_run_rules mirrors held natively. -fills is the mirror doing its job on an empty cell; -asserted-wins is the one that matters, because a mirror that overwrites an asserted cell silently replaces what the modeller SAID with what the engine INFERRED, and the answer being the existing rows unchanged is what forbids it. -nothing-derived pins the arm where the cell is empty and the mirror produced nothing, which must answer empty rather than bottom -- a store can simply lack the fact type. -both-empty is the same arm reached from the other side.",
DEF("case:mirror-fills", S2(A("derive:mirror_fill"),
    S2(S2(S2(A("r1"), A("Noun")), S2(A("r2"), A("Noun"))), PHI()))),
DEF("case:mirror-asserted-wins", S2(A("derive:mirror_fill"),
    S2(S2(S2(A("r1"), A("Noun")), S2(A("r2"), A("Noun"))),
       S1(S2(A("kept"), A("Asserted")))))),
DEF("case:mirror-nothing-derived", S2(A("derive:mirror_fill"),
    S2(PHI(), PHI()))),
"system:row_validate is LIVE in engine/rust (main.rs 9394) and was reached by no case and no law on any station -- meaning that runs and that nothing held four evaluators to. It BUILDS a validator rather than answering a verdict, and the modality picks which one: alethic (T) composes not-null over the value-constraint application, so a row is valid when the constraint MATCHES, while deontic (F) answers a constant F arm instead, because a deontic constraint is violable and must never refuse the row. The two cases are the two arms, and they must differ -- a builder that ignored the modality would pass a case that only checked the alethic side, and would silently make every deontic constraint refuse.",
"system:view_detail is the third of engine/rust's live-but-unreached system DEFs (main.rs 13538), and it is the UI surface: the detail view of one entity, built from its field pairs. -pairs is the ordinary shape and -empty is the arm that matters, because a detail view of an entity with no fields must still BE a detail view -- answering phi instead would give the renderer nothing to dispatch on, and that arm is exactly what a case reaching the DEF only through a populated store would never take.",
"system:explain is the last of engine/rust's four live-but-unreached system DEFs (main.rs 14478). It BUILDS the explanation chain for one derived row rather than answering it: engine/rust applies the built function to the store on the next line, and the comment there says the canonical chain CORROBORATES the host walk. A corroborator nothing holds to a station is worth exactly as much as the walk it was meant to check, which is why this belongs in the table.",
"theta:Restrict is Codd's restriction R_L|M S (2.1.5), the semijoin keeping rows of R whose L-key occurs in the M-projection of S -- and it was reached by no case and no law on any station, while engine/python calls it from canon.py. A named operation of the source algebra, live, and held to nothing. Like theta:Project it BUILDS the operator from its column lists rather than answering rows, so what the cases pin is the construction: -asym uses DIFFERENT columns on the two sides, which is the whole point of the operation carrying both L and M, and a builder that used L for both would answer identically to -sym and differ here.",
"ast:Purge and ast:DefineIn are Backus store operators that engine/rust calls and no case or law reached. Purge BUILDS the filter that drops every cell of a name -- the complement of ast:Fetch, and the one operation that can lose data, so a station disagreeing about it loses different data. DefineIn builds a store-into-a-named-substore, which is 14.7's nesting: a cell in one store may contain another entire store. Both are builders, so what is pinned is the construction.",
"constraints:scoped_external_uniqueness is a CONSTRAINT BUILDER engine/python calls (engine.py 779) that no case or law reached. Constraints are the half of a model that says no, so four evaluators free to disagree about one disagree about which populations are legal -- and a store that one host accepts and another refuses is worse than either answer. -one and -two differ only in the COLUMN LIST, which is the parameter the whole scoping rests on: a builder that ignored cols would answer identically for both and would enforce uniqueness over the wrong projection while looking like it worked.",
"system:mint_next builds the surrogate minter -- the successor of a max-fold over an id column -- and engine/python calls it (engine.py 944) while no case or law reached it. Of everything still unpinned this is the one whose disagreement cannot be undone: a wrong join can be recomputed and a wrong constraint can be re-checked, but two hosts that mint DIFFERENT ids for the same entity have written two different identities into the store, and nothing downstream can tell which was meant. -col1 and -col2 differ only in the column selector, so a builder that ignored it would mint from whatever column happened to be first. The empty arm rides inside the built form: null answers CONST 1, which is what makes the first mint into an empty population 1 rather than bottom.",
"system:resolve_minting is mint_next's other half (engine.py 951, unreached like it): mint_next answers WHAT the next id is, resolve_minting puts it INTO the row. Casing them together is the point -- a minter that agrees on the number and a resolver that places it at a different position still writes different facts, and pinning only the arithmetic would have left the placement free. -col1 and -col2 differ in the selector for the same reason mint_next's do.",
"system:checked_apply builds the TYPED BOUNDARY application (engine.py 3680, unreached): args and D apply a registered name only if every argument at a declared domain position is an instance of its object type, else the ERROR atom the transition rule refuses. Two failure directions matter and they are not symmetric -- an undeclared name applies UNCHECKED, while a signature naming an absent type cell FAILS CLOSED. A host that got those the wrong way round would either refuse everything unregistered or admit anything whose type cell went missing, and the second is the one that lets bad data in quietly. -a and -b differ by the name so the builder is shown to carry it rather than closing over a fixed one.",
DEF("case:nav-edges", S2(A("system:nav"),
    S2(A("Person"),
       S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("role"),
             S2(S4(A("r1"), A("F1"), N(1), A("Person")),
                S4(A("r2"), A("F1"), N(2), A("Company")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("factType"), S1(S1(A("F1")))),
          S3(A("CELL"), A("instanceOf"),
             S2(S2(A("Person"), A("ObjectType")),
                S2(A("Company"), A("ObjectType")))))))),
DEF("case:checked-apply-a", S2(A("system:checked_apply"), A("noop"))),
DEF("case:checked-apply-b", S2(A("system:checked_apply"), A("escape_html"))),
DEF("case:resolve-minting-col1", S2(A("system:resolve_minting"), N(1))),
DEF("case:resolve-minting-col2", S2(A("system:resolve_minting"), N(2))),
DEF("case:mint-next-col1", S2(A("system:mint_next"), N(1))),
DEF("case:mint-next-col2", S2(A("system:mint_next"), N(2))),
DEF("case:scoped-extuniq-one", S2(A("constraints:scoped_external_uniqueness"),
    S2(A("Enrolment"), S1(N(1))))),
DEF("case:scoped-extuniq-two", S2(A("constraints:scoped_external_uniqueness"),
    S2(A("Enrolment"), S2(N(1), N(2))))),
DEF("case:ast-purge", S2(A("ast:Purge"), A("Bob"))),
DEF("case:ast-definein", S2(A("ast:DefineIn"), S2(A("FILE"), A("Ada")))),
DEF("case:restrict-sym", S2(A("theta:Restrict"), S2(S1(N(1)), S1(N(1))))),
DEF("case:restrict-asym", S2(A("theta:Restrict"), S2(S1(N(1)), S1(N(2))))),
DEF("case:explain-builds", S2(A("system:explain"),
    S2(A("reaches"), S2(A("a"), A("b"))))),
"system:view_list and system:view_entry complete the view family beside view_detail, and were unreached the same way. All three tag a sequence -- list, entry, detail -- and the tag is what the renderer dispatches on, so three DEFs differing only in a constant is exactly the shape where a copy-paste slip produces a working-looking view of the wrong kind. Casing them together is what makes the tags checkable against each other, and it immediately paid: view_entry's element is CONS(input, 1, 4, 2), so it selects position FOUR and BOTTOMS on the two-wide pairs view_list and view_detail accept. Three DEFs with identical outer structure and incompatible element shapes -- which is the opposite of what reading the outer form suggests, and exactly why a case per DEF beats a case for the family. The empty arms are here for the reason view_detail's was: a list of nothing is still a LIST, and answering phi would leave the renderer with no tag at all.",
"system:cmp_filter_lit and system:cmp_filter_col are a comparison filter built two ways, and both were unreached. Given the SAME operand they must not answer the same thing: lit wraps the third element in CONST, so the column is compared against a LITERAL VALUE, while col uses it as a SELECTOR, so the column is compared against ANOTHER COLUMN. Cased on one operand for exactly that reason -- the two answers differ only by the CONST, and a host that picked the wrong one would compare a column against the NUMBER 2 where the rule meant column 2, or the reverse, and every row would still get a verdict. A filter that is wrong in this way does not fail, it just quietly selects the wrong rows.",
"system:enum_product is the cartesian product over enumeration domains -- INSERT of ep_step seeded with a single EMPTY tuple -- and engine/python names it among the DEFs the induce path runs (protocol.py 2691). Unreached. -two pins the ordinary case and the ORDER within it, since a product that varied the wrong factor fastest would answer the same SET and a different sequence, and the induce path ranks candidates positionally. -empty is the arm worth arguing about: no factors must yield ONE empty combination, not zero, because the product of nothing is the unit and a fold seeded with phi instead of a single empty tuple answers phi for every input. That is the seed, visible only when the input is empty.",
"system:role_domain answers a noun's induce domain -- the declared enum literals first, then everything else observed at the noun's role positions, later legs appending only what is absent so far. engine/python already pins it against induce_domain (test_induce_oracle 85), but that differential is python against python: it shows the host and its own canon evaluation agree and says nothing about the other three stations. This case is the missing half, and the ORDER is the property, since cat puts the declared literals ahead of the observed ones and a host that concatenated the other way answers the same SET with the enum no longer leading.",
"system:ctx_of answers the model context as four parts at once over the STORE ALONE, not a noun and a store. I first fed it a noun-and-store pair the way system:nav takes one and got four EMPTY components: not bottom, so it looked like it ran, while pinning the tuple shape and saying nothing about any content. The operand shape is what the case turns on and the ANSWER is what reveals it. Getting all four live then meant reading what each part consumes rather than guessing at a store: ctx_edges reads SUBTYPE rows, and ctx_vals filters instanceOf to ValueType, so a store of object types and fact types alone leaves both empty however rich it looks. All four are populated here and the two that could be confused are visibly distinct -- ctx_names carries every declared name including Amount, ctx_vals carries only Amount -- so a host answering names where values belong would show as a longer fourth component rather than passing.",
"system:ctx_subject is the subject resolver beside ctx_of: the leading declared Object Type of a reading, LONGEST MATCH FIRST, else the first word. Its own canon note calls the empty-candidate branch load-bearing and gives the number -- without it every unmatched reading answered bottom, 203 of the first 400 -- so -none is not a tidy edge case here, it is the majority path on real input. -longest offers both Person and Person Name against a reading that starts with both, and the longer must win; a resolver taking the first hit rather than the longest answers Person and silently subjects the reading to the wrong type. -none has candidates that match nothing, and -empty has no candidates at all, which are different routes to the same fallback and the second is the one INSERT bottoms on.",
"system:as_of BUILDS the as-of-time reader for a cell: fetch the cell, keep rows whose first column is at or before the given time, and drop that column. Operand is the cell NAME and the TIME, not a store -- the built function is applied to the store elsewhere, the same division as theta:Restrict and cmp_filter. I first handed it a store and got a four-kilobyte answer with the whole store frozen inside as a CONST: not empty, so the usual is-it-vacuous check passed it, and it was still the wrong case. For a BUILDER the check is whether the operand appears as a PARAMETER of the form rather than swallowed as a constant. -t2 and -t5 differ only in the time, which must show up inside the le comparison; a builder ignoring it would answer the same reader for every instant and silently make as-of-time mean now.",
"system:membership_def is the type-membership test the typed boundary rests on -- is this value an instance of that type -- and engine/python names it (engine.py 1007) while no case reached it. It reads its operands from the SECOND component, the index cell and then the value, which is why the first is unused here rather than missing. Both verdicts are cased because a membership test that answered T for everything would admit any argument at any declared domain position, and system:checked_apply, cased earlier in this session, is built directly on top of it: the boundary refuses exactly what this says is not a member, so the two agree or the boundary is decoration.",
"system:clause_ft resolves a constraint clause to the fact type it references, and engine/python's _clause_ft is a thin caller over it (compiler.py 1048). No case reached it. Its operand is FOUR parts -- the normalized text, the declared names, the stop words, and the declared FACT TYPES -- and I found that by instrumenting rather than reading, after a two-part guess and a three-part one both answered bottom. The fourth part is the one that matters and the pair below is built on it: the SAME text and the SAME names resolve to Employee_is_manager when the fact types are absent and Employee_is_a_manager when they are present. The docstring names that difference as the defect that shaped this DEF -- stripping the article resolved the clause to a cell that does not exist, and a constraint pointing at a missing cell is not an error, it is SILENTLY UNENFORCED. There is a second reason it needed casing, which compiler.py states outright: once the host became a thin caller its corpus oracle COMPARES CANON TO ITSELF and can no longer catch drift. Delegating to canon removed the check that the delegation was right, and the case table is where that check has to live now.",
"system:cs_rows turns a set-comparison statement into the constraint A-row plus its attachment rows, and engine/python's handlers are thin callers over it (compiler.py 1099). Unreached. The KIND is the first operand and it does not vary a detail, it picks the constraint FAMILY: at most builds an exclusion over the subject, subset of builds an equality over the clause fact types, and the two answers share almost no structure. A host that mapped a kind to the wrong family would emit a well-formed constraint of the wrong sort, which enforces something the model never said rather than failing. Both kinds are cased for that reason, and the operand was found by instrumenting the DEF rather than reading its head -- five parts, kind and subject and clause ids and raw texts and modality.",
"system:cand_score sums the confidence scores belonging to ONE hypothesis, and engine/python's induce path calls it (protocol.py 2744) while no case reached it. -mixed is the property: rows for h1 and h2 are present and the answer is 7, the h1 rows alone, not 12. A scorer that summed the whole cell would rank every candidate identically and the induce ranking would still produce an order -- just an arbitrary one, which is the failure that looks like it works. -absent has rows but none for this hypothesis and -empty has no rows at all; both answer 0, which is the fold's seed and the reason an unscored candidate ranks last rather than bottoming the judge.",
"system:absorb_core is the absorption fold -- INSERT of theta:NatJoin on a shared key, the variadic natural join that assembles a wide row from the populations absorbed into one table. engine/python calls it and keeps only the D-access override (engine.py 2600). Unreached. -two shows the join DROPPING the rows that do not match on the key, which is the half a union would get wrong while still answering rows. -one is the fold over a single population and must be the IDENTITY: a fold that joined the lone population against its seed would answer the same shape with the wrong arity, and every absorbed one-fact-type table in the base takes that path. -three shows the variadic widening, which is what makes this a fold rather than a binary join used twice.",
"ast:build_system assembles the create handler from its record -- cell name, validate, resolve, derive, links, machine, mealy, index, append -- and engine/rust reduces the result to run a transition (main.rs 9658). Unreached. The record's slots are OPTIONAL and empty here, which is the point: the minimal record is the one every fact type without a machine or a mealy or an index takes, so it is the common path rather than a stripped-down curiosity. The cell name rides through as a parameter, ('CONST', 'create:F1') inside the apndl, which is the builder check -- swallowed as a constant would mean the record was ignored. ONE case, not a pair: the built form is five kilobytes and a second would double that in the table to show the same parameterization the embedded name already shows.",
"ast:SYSTEM is METACOMPOSITION -- Backus 13.3.2, the mechanism the paper says is the only one it has. Fetch the handler NAMED in the operand out of the store, then apply it to the argument and the store. engine/rust runs the command pipeline through it and no case or law on any station reached it, which means the dispatch every command in this system passes through was held by nothing. The pair says it in one line: the SAME store and the SAME argument, differing only in the NAME, run different handlers -- h1 is a CONST and answers ok, h2 is a selector and answers p. A host that fetched the wrong cell, or fetched the right cell and applied it to the wrong operand, would still answer something for both; only two names over one store distinguishes dispatch from computation.",
"system:propose_report is what a proposal WOULD add -- sort_asc over theta:setminus of the after and before populations -- and engine/python calls it with a sorted set difference as its fallback (protocol.py 3015). Unreached. The SORT is not presentation here: the fallback the host keeps beside it is sorted(set(after) - set(before)), so an unsorted answer diverges from the path the host takes when canon is not used, and the two would disagree about the ORDER of a proposal a person is about to approve. -unsorted feeds c,a,b and must answer b,c. -all-removed is the arm where the proposal adds nothing and must answer empty rather than bottom, which is what lets a no-op proposal be reported rather than crash the report.",
"system:cand_gate admits an induce candidate only if adding it introduces NO NEW VIOLATION -- null of the setminus between the violations with the candidate and the violations without it. I recorded this DEF as UNCASEABLE two commits ago, on the grounds that its third operand is a built validator function and a case is a function name applied to a data literal. That was WRONG and the correction is the useful part: cand_violations applies the validator to the rows and the store and takes element TWO of the answer, so any term literal answering a pair serves, and CONS(2,1) makes the violations BE the rows. The validator does not have to be the real one to pin the gate; it has to make the two sides differ. -blocked adds a candidate absent from the existing rows, so the setminus is non-empty and the gate refuses. -admits adds one already present, so nothing is new and it passes. A gate that ignored the difference and answered T throughout would admit every candidate the induce path ever proposes.",
"system:cand_covers is the LAST of the live-but-unreached DEFs, and it asks whether every target the candidate must cover is present in its cell -- ALPHA of cand_covers_one, then filter to the F answers, then null. -partial is the case that carries it: one target covered and one not must answer F, because a covers-check reading ANY instead of ALL passes both single-element cases and only diverges when the targets disagree. That is the same shape as the longest-match arm in ctx_subject and the two-noun arm in instance_mirror -- a quantifier confusion is invisible until two elements disagree. The store's rows are BARE ATOMS here rather than one-tuples, which is not decoration: cand_covers_one compares the target value against the row directly, so a store of one-tuples answers F for everything and would have made -covered look like a working negative case.",
"system:verify_store is the audit: every destructive head must REPRODUCE, meaning the rows stored in its cell are exactly what re-running its rules answers. It is a declared certified twin -- engine/python carries the host override and OVERRIDES names it -- and it was the last DEF any host calls that no case or law reached. A twin declared and uncased is the narrower of the two failures, but it is still one: the registry says the host copy and the DEF agree, and nothing was checking that the DEF itself agreed across stations. Reaching it needed four cells and a rule that RESOLVES: ruleDerives names the head's rule, passHeads puts the head in a destructive pass so audit_heads selects it, the head's own cell holds the stored rows, and the rule id must name a real DEF because audit_recompute APPLIES it -- a store cell will not do, which is why an invented r1 answers bottom. system:audit_destr serves as the rule here precisely because its answer over this store is a known single row, so -match stores exactly that and -mismatch stores it plus one more. Both arms matter: an audit that answered T unconditionally would report every stored cell as reproducing, which is the failure an audit exists to prevent.",
DEF("case:verify-store-match", S2(A("system:verify_store"),
    S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("ruleDerives"), S1(S2(A("system:audit_destr"), A("H1")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("passHeads"), S1(S2(A("dred"), A("H1")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("H1"), S1(A("H1")))))),
DEF("case:verify-store-mismatch", S2(A("system:verify_store"),
    S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("ruleDerives"), S1(S2(A("system:audit_destr"), A("H1")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("passHeads"), S1(S2(A("dred"), A("H1")))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("H1"), S2(A("H1"), A("ZZ")))))),
